  "enhanced_information": "In the case of a deep second degree flame burn covering 50% of body surface area, the following measures are evaluated: 1) Absolute diet is not indicated as patients require nutritional support, but this is typically provided through enteral feeding or IV nutrition rather than an absolute diet. 2) Administration of lactate ringer according to the Parkland formula is indicated for fluid resuscitation in burn patients. 3) Prophylactic administration of intravenous antibiotics is not universally indicated; while there is a high risk of infection, antibiotics are typically reserved for confirmed infections rather than used prophylactically. 4) Heparin administration for antithrombotic prophylaxis is often recommended due to the high risk of venous thromboembolism in patients with extensive burns. Therefore, the measure that would NOT be indicated is the absolute diet, as it contradicts the need for appropriate nutritional support in burn management."
